## Short form

<Two paragraphs, under 150 words together. The first opens "For …
<subsystem> is …"; the second opens "Unlike …" and ends with the trade,
as a sentence of its own ("The cost is …"). Every house term gets a
footnote gloss the first time it appears — `[^term]`, defined at the end of
the page in plain words an outsider can follow. Write for a reader who has
not opened the repo: no counts, no internal nouns without a gloss, one idea
per sentence.>

## Full form

**For** <the target customer: a role, not a company type>, who <the need
that makes them look for something in this category>,

**<subsystem> is** <the category, in words a reader from outside the
project would use>: <what it is made of — the mechanisms, not adjectives>,

**that** <the one key benefit, and the clauses that make it concrete; each
clause maps to a row of the table below>,

**unlike** <the primary alternative a real team would pick; name it as a
practice or a product class, not a straw man>,

**<subsystem>** <the one primary difference — one, not a list>. The cost
is <what the customer gives up>.

<The full form stays under about 200 words: one idea per slot, no semicolon
lists. Inventories belong in the table below, not in the clauses.>

## What each clause rests on

Every clause above is a projection of a premise or a decision. A clause with
no row is either cut or marked *proposed*.

| Slot | Clause | Rests on |
| --- | --- | --- |
| For | … | <a why-boxer premise (P1–P7), an ADR's Context> |
| is a | … | <ADR-NNNN …> |
| that | … | <ADR-NNNN …> |
| unlike | … | <the ADR whose Alternatives section rejects it, with the kill-reason> |
| <subsystem> | … | <ADR-NNNN …> |
| trade | … | <the ADR's Consequences, or "What this costs you" in why-boxer> |

